course: dictionary
lesson: 6537
title: Neutralisation
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Neutralisation is a chemical reaction. An acid and an alkali meet, react, and cancel each other out. «When an acid meets an alkali, they react and cancel each other out: this is called neutralisation.» The reaction makes a salt and water, so the acid and alkali are no longer strong.
explain: Look at the clues in the sentence. «Alkalis react with acids and cancel them out; this is called neutralisation.» Cancel out means stop each other's effect. You can test this by swapping in your meaning: neutralisation is the act of cancelling out, so it fits.
explain: The same idea is used in everyday life. An antacid tablet neutralises stomach acid, and farmers add lime to acid soil. The word also has a wider meaning: making something have no effect, or stopping a danger.
fact: Neutralisation is a chemical reaction between an acid and an alkali.
fact: The general formula is acid + alkali → salt + water.
fact: Neutral means pH 7, where acid and alkali are balanced; indicator paper turns green at neutral.
fact: An antacid tablet neutralises stomach acid, which helps with indigestion.
fact: Farmers add lime to acid soil to neutralise it, and toothpaste can also be used to neutralise acids in the mouth.
exercise: «Alkalis react with acids and cancel them out; this is called neutralisation.» What is neutralisation in this sentence? | Making a loud noise | Dissolving sugar in water | Heating a metal until it melts | *Acid and alkali cancelling each other out
exercise: «When an acid meets an alkali, they react and cancel each other out: this is called neutralisation.» What two things are made in neutralisation? | Sugar and oil | Smoke and ash | Gold and air | *A salt and water
exercise: Farmers add lime to acid soil. Why do they do this? | *To cancel out the acid in the soil | To stop the rain | To make the soil taste sweet | To turn the soil into glass
test: «When an acid meets an alkali, they react and cancel each other out: this is called neutralisation.» Which two things meet in neutralisation? | Two alkalis only | Water and sugar | Salt and pepper | *An acid and an alkali
test: Indicator paper turns green at neutral. On the pH scale, what number is neutral? | Zero | *pH 7 | pH 1 | pH 14
exam: An antacid tablet cancels out stomach acid. Which word names this process? | Combustion | Distillation | *Neutralisation | Erosion
